    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While there are claims that the retention of such artifacts by museums mirrors a willingness to hold onto culturally significant items acquired via colonialism, it is also crucial to explore legitimate arguments for retention. Universal museums often argue that they provide a global context for these artifacts, preserving them in a controlled environment and making them accessible to an international audience. Further academic discussion around repatriation considers the abilities of countries or communities to adequately care for, display, and interpret these pieces. This policy seeks to engage in that discourse, emphasizing a cooperative approach towards repatriation that respects indigenous heritage while acknowledging the educational role our museum can continue to play.",
      "source": "target",
